\",\"name\":\"GreenPoint Maintenance Services Corp\",\"url\":\"https://greenpointms.com\"},\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://greenpointms.com/blog/medical-office-cleaning-requirements-osha/\",\"keywords\":\"medical office cleaning requirements, OSHA cleaning standards, healthcare cleaning compliance\"}"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://greenpointms.com\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Blog\",\"item\":\"https://greenpointms.com/blog/\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Medical Office Cleaning Requirements: OSHA Compliance Guide\"}]}"}}],["$","$L2",null,{}],["$","section",null,{"style":{"background":"#0A2A16","paddingTop":140,"paddingBottom":64,"position":"relative","overflow":"hidden"},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"position":"absolute","inset":0,"opacity":0.04,"backgroundImage":"linear-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,0.1) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255,255,255,0.1) 1px, transparent 1px)","backgroundSize":"60px 60px","pointerEvents":"none"}}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":720,"margin":"0 auto","padding":"0 24px","position":"relative","zIndex":1},"children":[["$","nav",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","gap":8,"alignItems":"center","marginBottom":24,"flexWrap":"wrap"},"children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","textDecoration":"none","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Home"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)","fontSize":12},"children":"›"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog/","style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","textDecoration":"none","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Blog"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)","fontSize":12},"children":"›"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"#C8A34D","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Compliance"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":12,"marginBottom":20,"flexWrap":"wrap"},"children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"background":"rgba(200,163,77,0.15)","color":"#C8A34D","padding":"4px 10px","borderRadius":4,"fontSize":11,"fontWeight":600,"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Compliance"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontSize":12,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace"},"children":"May 26, 2025"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontSize":12,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace"},"children":["· ","9 min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":"clamp(28px, 4vw, 42px)","fontWeight":700,"color":"#fff","lineHeight":1.15,"marginBottom":0,"letterSpacing":"-0.02em"},"children":"Medical Office Cleaning Requirements: OSHA Compliance Guide"}]]}]]}],"$undefined",["$","article",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":720,"margin":"0 auto","padding":"48px 24px 64px"},"children":[[["$","p","0",{"style":{"fontSize":18,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":32,"fontWeight":500},"children":"Medical office cleaning isn't just about aesthetics — it's a regulatory requirement with real consequences for non-compliance. OSHA's Bloodborne Pathogens Standard (29 CFR 1910.1030), along with state health department regulations, establishes specific requirements for how healthcare environments must be cleaned and disinfected. This guide covers the essential compliance requirements every medical office manager needs to understand."}],["$","h2","1",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"OSHA Bloodborne Pathogen Standards for Cleaning"}],["$","p","2",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"OSHA requires that all equipment and surfaces contaminated with blood or other potentially infectious materials (OPIM) be cleaned and decontaminated. This applies to exam tables, countertops, floors, and any surface that may come into contact with blood or bodily fluids. The standard requires the use of EPA-registered hospital-grade disinfectants, proper personal protective equipment (PPE) for cleaning staff, documented cleaning schedules and procedures, and immediate cleanup of spills involving blood or OPIM."}],["$","h2","3",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"Terminal Cleaning vs. Routine Cleaning"}],["$","p","4",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"Routine cleaning happens on a regular schedule — daily trash removal, restroom sanitization, and general surface cleaning. Terminal cleaning is the thorough, top-to-bottom decontamination of a clinical space. In medical offices, terminal cleaning should occur after each patient in procedure rooms, at the end of each day for all exam rooms, and after any known or suspected contamination event. Terminal cleaning includes all horizontal and vertical surfaces, equipment exteriors, light switches, door handles, and any surface within the patient care zone."}],["$","h2","5",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"EPA-Registered Disinfectants"}],["$","p","6",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"Not all cleaning products meet healthcare standards. Medical offices must use EPA-registered disinfectants with demonstrated efficacy against relevant pathogens. For broad-spectrum protection, products should be effective against MRSA, VRE, norovirus, influenza, and tuberculosis. Dwell time — the amount of time a disinfectant must remain wet on a surface to be effective — is critical. Most hospital-grade disinfectants require 1-10 minutes of wet contact time. Wiping a surface and immediately drying it provides zero disinfection regardless of the product used."}],["$","h2","7",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"High-Touch Surface Protocols"}],["$","p","8",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"CDC guidelines identify high-touch surfaces as the primary vectors for healthcare-associated infections. In medical offices, critical high-touch surfaces include door handles and push plates, light switches, exam table surfaces and rails, computer keyboards and mice, phone handsets, faucet handles, chair armrests in waiting areas, and elevator buttons. These surfaces should be disinfected multiple times daily, not just during after-hours cleaning."}],["$","h2","9",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"Documentation and Compliance Records"}],["$","p","10",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"OSHA inspectors expect documentation. Your cleaning program should include written standard operating procedures for each area, training records for all cleaning staff, cleaning logs with dates, times, and personnel, product safety data sheets (SDS) for all chemicals used, incident reports for any exposure events, and quality verification records such as ATP testing results. Digital systems like JaniTrack make compliance documentation automatic — every cleaning event is photographed, timestamped, and stored for inspection readiness."}],["$","h2","11",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"Common Compliance Failures"}],["$","p","12",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"The most frequent OSHA citations in healthcare cleaning involve insufficient dwell time for disinfectants (staff wiping too quickly), lack of PPE for cleaning staff handling contaminated materials, no written exposure control plan, failure to use EPA-registered products (using household cleaners instead), and inadequate training documentation. These failures are entirely preventable with proper protocols and a cleaning partner experienced in healthcare environments."}],["$","div","13",{"style":{"background":"#F0F7F2","borderLeft":"3px solid #1B7A3D","padding":"20px 24px","borderRadius":"0 8px 8px 0","margin":"32px 0"},"children":["$","p",null,{"style":{"fontSize":15,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.7,"fontWeight":500,"margin":0},"children":"If your current cleaning company can't explain their bloodborne pathogen protocols, name the EPA registration numbers of their disinfectants, or show you documented dwell time procedures — it's time to switch. Compliance isn't optional in healthcare."}]}]],["$","div",null,{"style":{"borderTop":"1px solid #E2EBE5","marginTop":48,"paddingTop":32,"display":"flex","flexDirection":"column","gap":16},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":14},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"width":48,"height":48,"borderRadius":8,"background":"#1B7A3D","display":"flex","alignItems":"center","justifyContent":"center","color":"#fff","fontWeight":800,"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":20},"children":"G"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"fontWeight":600,"color":"#1A2B1F","fontSize":15},"children":"GreenPoint Maintenance Services"}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"fontSize":13,"color":"#8A9B91"},"children":"MBE-Certified Commercial Cleaning · NY, NJ, CT, PA, FL"}]]}]]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/#schedule","style":{"display":"inline-flex","alignItems":"center","gap":8,"background":"#1B7A3D","color":"#fff","padding":"12px 24px","borderRadius":8,"fontWeight":700,"fontSize":13,"textDecoration":"none","fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","letterSpacing":"0.04em","alignSelf":"flex-start"},"children":"Schedule a Free Walkthrough →"}]]}]]}],["$","section",null,{"style":{"background":"#F7FAF8","padding":"64px 24px","borderTop":"1px solid #E2EBE5"},"children":["$","div",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":900,"margin":"0 auto"},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":12,"marginBottom":24},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"height":1,"width":32,"background":"rgba(200,163,77,0.3)"}}],["$","h3",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":10,"color":"#C8A34D","fontWeight":700,"letterSpacing":"0.15em","textTransform":"uppercase","margin":0},"children":"Related Articles"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"grid","gridTemplateColumns":"repeat(auto-fill, minmax(260px, 1fr))","gap":16},"children":[["$","a","nyc-local-law-building-maintenance",{"href":"/blog/nyc-local-law-building-maintenance/","className":"blog-related","style":{"display":"block","textDecoration":"none","background":"#FFFFFF","borderRadius":10,"border":"1px solid #E2EBE5","padding":20,"transition":"transform 0.2s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease"},"children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontSize":11,"color":"#8A9B91","fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontWeight":600},"children":"Compliance"}],["$","h4",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":16,"fontWeight":700,"